+# Decision: Represent Concurrent Visit Edits as Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers can remain offline for 48 hours and may independently correct the same visit after comparing field notes. By the time a tablet submits an edit, another edit derived from the same starting state may already be current. Arrival order does not establish which scientific observation is authoritative, and silently replacing either submission would make the exported record untrustworthy.
+
+A visit contains fields whose scientific meaning can cross structural boundaries. A changed species identification may affect its cover estimate, notes may qualify several observations, and observer corrections may explain other changes. The record store needs a concurrency policy that preserves submissions, determines when coordinator review is required, and gives tablet-sync and review-export a stable contract.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+### Gates
+
+- **No submitted edit is silently discarded.** Any viable approach must retain every accepted original submission and its provenance. This inherits the HLD tenet "Do not silently discard a submitted edit."
+- **Exported values remain inspectable.** Any viable approach must preserve enough history to explain how each exported visit followed from submitted observations and coordinator action. This inherits the HLD tenet "Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable."
+- **Offline work does not require coordination.** A tablet must be able to edit from its locally retained starting state without locking the visit or contacting the server. This inherits the HLD tenet "Let field workers continue collecting observations without connectivity."
+
+### Weighted Criteria
+
+- **Scientific coherence of an edit: major.** The policy should avoid constructing a visit whose fields were individually valid but were never considered together by either a worker or coordinator.
+- **Operational clarity: major.** Two coordinators should be able to understand why a visit needs attention and what action makes it exportable, consistent with the tenet "Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator."
+- **Review volume: moderate.** Automatic handling of demonstrably independent changes is valuable because each flagged visit consumes coordinator time before the Friday export.
+- **Rule maintenance: moderate.** The policy should limit concurrency rules that must evolve whenever visit fields or scientific relationships change.
+- **Offline payload and state cost: minor.** The first release handles structured visit records at modest scale, so complete payloads or base snapshots are feasible, but unnecessary complexity on shared tablets still has a cost.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+**Description.** Each accepted edit is an immutable, complete visit revision naming the revision from which it was derived. An edit based on the current revision advances the visit when no review is pending. A stale-base edit becomes a competing revision. The current revision remains available, but the visit is withheld from export until a coordinator compares the complete alternatives and selects one or authors a complete replacement revision.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- **Passes: no submitted edit is silently discarded.** Every accepted payload remains an immutable revision whether or not it becomes current.
+- **Passes: exported values remain inspectable.** Revision ancestry and the coordinator's resolution record connect the exported whole visit to the complete submissions considered.
+- **Passes: offline work does not require coordination.** A tablet needs only a base revision ID, a complete edited visit, and an idempotency key when it reconnects.
+- **Strong: scientific coherence of an edit.** Automatic processing preserves each worker's fields as a unit; only a person deliberately creates a visit that combines competing observations.
+- **Strong: operational clarity.** Review presents complete alternatives and has one visit-level outcome, without exposing a merge engine's intermediate decisions.
+- **Weak: review volume.** Unrelated changes derived from the same base still create review work.
+- **Strong: rule maintenance.** The concurrency rule is independent of field type and does not grow with the visit schema.
+- **Partial: offline payload and state cost.** Tablets submit complete records but retain only revision IDs rather than mergeable base snapshots.
+
+### Field-Aware Three-Way Merge
+
+**Description.** Each tablet retains the base snapshot and submits structured changes. The server compares the base, current state, and incoming changes. Rules for scalar fields, observer lists, species entries, and notes automatically combine disjoint edits; overlapping edits become review work. Each resulting field retains provenance identifying the submission or coordinator action that supplied it.
+
+This option remains credible when coordinator capacity becomes the binding constraint, visit volume or concurrent correction frequency rises materially, or the domain develops stable rules that can identify independent edits without crossing scientific relationships. Under those conditions, automatic handling of unrelated changes could protect the Friday export schedule better than visit-level review.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- **Passes: no submitted edit is silently discarded.** Original submissions can remain immutable even when their changes feed a combined representation.
+- **Passes: exported values remain inspectable.** Field-level provenance and merge records can explain the source of each value, provided every merge rule records its outcome.
+- **Passes: offline work does not require coordination.** Tablets can submit after disconnection if they retain the complete base snapshot needed for comparison.
+- **Weak: scientific coherence of an edit.** Structurally disjoint changes can still be scientifically related, and the server lacks enough domain understanding to recognize every relationship.
+- **Partial: operational clarity.** Coordinators see fewer conflicts but must understand field-level provenance, automatic combinations, and overlap rules when investigating a visit.
+- **Strong: review volume.** Disjoint edits can become exportable without coordinator action.
+- **Weak: rule maintenance.** Every collection-valued or newly added field needs explicit merge semantics, and rules must evolve with scientific practice.
+- **Weak: offline payload and state cost.** Tablets retain base snapshots and submit structured changes rather than a base identifier and complete replacement payload.
+
+## Selection
+
+The record store uses whole-visit revisions. Scientific coherence and operational clarity outweigh the moderate benefit of reducing review volume at the project's current scale. This selection turns most directly on the HLD tenets "Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable" and "Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator." It also avoids introducing merge behavior that would appear precise while lacking the scientific context needed to decide whether two fields are genuinely independent.
+
+The choice accepts a larger coordinator queue and complete-visit submission payloads. It forecloses automatic combination of concurrent edits in the first release, not future adoption of field-aware merging if measured review volume becomes unsustainable and maintainable domain rules emerge.
+
+Tablet-sync must retain the base revision ID for each editable visit, submit a complete payload with a stable idempotency key, and distinguish an accepted revision that became current from one held for review. It does not need to retain a mergeable base snapshot or calculate a structured field patch.
+
+Review-export must compare complete competing revisions, keep unresolved visits out of the CSV, and resolve the entire current conflict set atomically by selecting a revision or authoring a complete replacement. It needs visit-level provenance and resolution history rather than field-level merge provenance. Neither downstream component may promote a competing revision merely because it arrived last.
